WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.560
I can’t believe that I am saying but, but today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:15.280
we are going to see how NVIDIA is getting&nbsp;
closer and closer to solving an almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.720
impossible problem. And that is, writing&nbsp;
real-time light transport simulations.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:29.520
So, what is that? And why is it nearly impossible?&nbsp;
Well, if we wish to create a truly photorealistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.520
scene, in computer graphics, we usually reach&nbsp;
out to a light transport simulation algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:43.840
and then, this happens. Oh yes, concept number&nbsp;
one. Noise! This is not photorealistic at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:51.120
not yet anyway. Why is that? Well, during&nbsp;
this process, we have to shoot millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.960
and millions of light rays into the scene to&nbsp;
estimate how much light is bouncing around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:04.320
and before we have simulated enough rays, the&nbsp;
inaccuracies in our estimations show up as noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:12.320
in these images. This clears up over time, but it&nbsp;
may take from minutes to days for this to happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:21.280
even for a smaller scene. For instance, this one&nbsp;
took us 3 full weeks to finish. 3 weeks! Ouch.&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:30.400
Now, earlier, we talked about this technique which&nbsp;
could take complex geometry, and 3.4 million light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:37.360
sources, and it could really render not just&nbsp;
an image, but an animation of it interactively.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.440
But how? Well, the magic behind all this is&nbsp;
a smarter allocation of these ray samples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:51.200
that we have to shoot into the scene. For&nbsp;
instance, this technique does not forget what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:59.440
we did just a moment ago when we move the camera&nbsp;
a little and advance to the next image. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:06.080
lots of information that is otherwise thrown away&nbsp;
can now be reused as we advance the animation.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:13.520
And, note that even then, these smooth,&nbsp;
beautiful images are not what we get directly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.520
if we look under the hood and look at the&nbsp;
raw result that comes out of a simulation, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:24.080
get something like this. Oh yes.&nbsp;
Still, a noisy image. But wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:30.080
don’t despair! We don’t have to live with these&nbsp;
noisy images, we have denoising algorithms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.320
tailored for light simulations. This one does&nbsp;
some serious legwork with this noisy input.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:43.120
And, in a followup paper, they also went on to&nbsp;
tackle these notoriously difficult photorealistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:51.520
smoke plumes, volumetric bunnies, and even&nbsp;
explosions interactively. The results were once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:59.040
again, noise filtered to nearly perfection. Not to&nbsp;
perfection, but a step closer to it than before.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:06.640
Now note that I used the word interactively&nbsp;
twice here. I did not say real time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.920
And that is not by mistake. These&nbsp;
techniques are absolutely fantastic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.840
one of the bigger leaps in light&nbsp;
transport research, but, they still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.320
cost a touch more than what production systems&nbsp;
can shoulder. They are not quite real time yet.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.200
So, what did they do? Did they stop there? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.080
of course not, they rolled up the&nbsp;
sleeves and continued. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.240
I hope you know what’s coming. Oh yes! Have a&nbsp;
look at this newer paper they have in this area.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.800
This is their result on the Paris Opera House&nbsp;
scene, which is quite detailed, there is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:51.680
ton going on here. And, you are all experienced&nbsp;
Fellow Scholars now, so when you see them flicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:58.960
between the raw, noisy and the denoised results,&nbsp;
you now know exactly what is going on. And, hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:07.760
on to your papers, because all this takes about 12&nbsp;
milliseconds per frame. Yes yes yes! My goodness!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.920
That is finally in the real time domain&nbsp;
and then some! What a time to be alive!

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:22.000
Okay, so where is the catch? Our keen eyes see&nbsp;
that this is a static scene. It probably can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.240
deal with dynamic movements and rapid changes&nbsp;
in lighting, can it? Well, let’s have a look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:38.480
Wow! I cannot believe my eyes. Dynamic movement,&nbsp;
checkmark. And here, this is as much changing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:46.160
the lighting as anyone would ever want, and&nbsp;
it can do this too. I absolutely love it.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:52.000
And, remember the amusement park scene&nbsp;
from the previous paper? The one with 23&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.280
million triangles for the geometry,&nbsp;
and over 3 million light sources?&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:04.720
Well, here are the raw results, and after&nbsp;
denoising, this looks super clean. Wow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:11.200
So, how long do we have to wait for this?&nbsp;
This can’t be real time, right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:17.680
all this takes is about 12 milliseconds&nbsp;
per frame. Again. And this is where I fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.040
off the chair when reading this paper. Of&nbsp;
course, not even this technique is perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:29.200
the glossy reflections are a little blurry at&nbsp;
places, and artifacts in the lighting can still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.600
appear. But if this is not a quantum leap in&nbsp;
light transport research, I don’t know what is.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.960
Plus, if you wish to see some properly detailed&nbsp;
comparisons against previous techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.600
make sure to have a look at the paper. And,&nbsp;
if you have been holding on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.280
now, squeeze that paper, because&nbsp;
everything that you see in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.720
paper was done by two people. Huge&nbsp;
congratulations Chris and Alexey!

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.360
And, if you are wondering if we&nbsp;
ever get to use these techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.240
don’t forget that their marbles demo is&nbsp;
already out there for all of us to use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:13.520
And it gets better, for instance, not many know&nbsp;
that they already have a denoising technique that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:21.360
is online and ready to use for all of us. This&nbsp;
one is a professional grade tool right there. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:27.600
is really incredible, they have so many tools&nbsp;
out there for us to use, I check what NVIDIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:34.480
is up to daily, and I still quite often get&nbsp;
surprised about how much they have going on.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.040
So, finally, real time light&nbsp;
transport in our lifetimes?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:43.360
Oh yes, this paper is history in the making.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

